WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.650
Standard containers reduce many manual memory mistakes, but they do not make every access bounds-checked.

2
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:13.419
A container-backed design still needs clear size discipline, especially when indexes, counts, offsets, or lengths come from outside the trusted part

3
00:00:13.419 --> 00:00:14.478
of the program.

4
00:00:14.478 --> 00:00:21.188
size() tells you how many elements are present. capacity() tells you how much storage may be available without reallocating.

5
00:00:21.188 --> 00:00:22.953
Capacity is not initialized data.

6
00:00:22.953 --> 00:00:27.191
Code that treats capacity as available elements is making an unsafe assumption.

7
00:00:27.191 --> 00:00:31.428
operator[] can be appropriate when the surrounding logic has already proven bounds.

8
00:00:31.428 --> 00:00:37.785
The problem is not the operator itself; the problem is hiding an assumption that no reviewer can verify.

9
00:00:37.785 --> 00:00:43.788
When uncertainty remains, checked access such as at() or an explicit guard can make the contract clearer.

10
00:00:43.788 --> 00:00:48.379
Externally influenced sizes should be validated before allocation, indexing, copying, slicing, or transformation.

11
00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:57.207
A parsed number may fit in a type but still be too large for a container, too expensive to process, or inconsistent with another field.

12
00:00:57.207 --> 00:00:59.326
Signed and unsigned comparisons deserve review.

13
00:00:59.326 --> 00:01:05.329
Container sizes are commonly represented with unsigned types, while parsed inputs and loop indexes may be signed.

14
00:01:05.329 --> 00:01:10.626
Conversions should happen only after checking that values are nonnegative and within the intended range.

15
00:01:10.626 --> 00:01:13.098
Iterator ranges are another form of bounds.

16
00:01:13.098 --> 00:01:22.279
A half-open range should come from the same container or a clearly related view, and the end iterator should really be reachable from the begin

17
00:01:22.279 --> 00:01:22.632
iterator.

18
00:01:22.632 --> 00:01:26.517
Mismatched ranges can make an algorithm's contract impossible to satisfy.

19
00:01:26.517 --> 00:01:30.048
The defensive habit is to state the precondition before access.

20
00:01:30.048 --> 00:01:39.229
If code indexes, copies, reserves, or loops based on a value, reviewers should see where that value was checked and what limit the program is willing

21
00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:39.935
to support.

22
00:01:39.935 --> 00:01:42.760
Size discipline is also a resource-control practice.

23
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:49.470
Reserving or resizing a container based on input can allocate large amounts of memory before any element is processed.

24
00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:55.120
Validate both the raw size and any derived size calculation before asking a container to grow.
